L["jairus"] = """When Jesus came back across the lake, a large crowd gathered around him on the shore. Then a man named Jairus came. He was a leader of the synagogue, a respected man in his town. But he forgot his dignity. He fell at Jesus' feet and begged him again and again. My little daughter is very sick. Please come and put your hands on her so that she will be healed and live. She was twelve years old.

Jesus went with him, and a large crowd followed, pressing around him. In the crowd was a woman who had been sick for twelve years. She had spent everything she had on doctors, and instead of getting better, she had grown worse. Her condition also made her an outcast under the law. She had heard about Jesus. She thought, if I just touch his clothes, I will be healed. She came up behind him and touched his cloak, and immediately her illness was gone. She felt it in her body.

Jesus stopped. He realized that power had gone out from him. Who touched my clothes? His disciples said, you see the people crowding against you, and yet you ask, who touched me? But Jesus kept looking around. The woman, trembling with fear, came and fell at his feet and told him the whole truth. He said to her, daughter, your faith has healed you. Go in peace and be freed from your suffering. He called her daughter, the only time he is recorded addressing someone that way.

Meanwhile, Jairus was waiting. Every minute counted. While Jesus was still speaking, some people came from the house of Jairus with sad news. Your daughter is gone. Why bother the teacher anymore? Overhearing what they said, Jesus told him, don't be afraid. Just believe.

He let no one follow him except Peter, James and John. When they came to the home, Jesus saw a commotion, with people crying and wailing loudly. He went in and said, why all this commotion and wailing? The child is not dead but asleep. They laughed at him. After he put them all out, he took the child's father and mother and the disciples who were with him and went in where the child was.

He took her by the hand and said to her, talitha koum, which means, little girl, I say to you, get up. Immediately the girl stood up and began to walk around. She was twelve years old. At this they were completely astonished. He gave strict orders not to let anyone know about this, and told them to give her something to eat.

Why does this matter? The two stories are woven together on purpose. A powerful man and a nameless outcast woman both needed Jesus. He gave time and attention to each of them. And the number twelve ties them together, a girl of twelve years and a woman who had suffered for twelve years. Jesus has power over illness and over the worst thing we fear.

What happened after? Jesus kept teaching and healing. The woman's story would be told wherever the gospel was preached. Later, Jesus would raise his friend Lazarus, and then rise himself.

What does this mean for us today? Jairus had to keep trusting while he waited, even when the interruption seemed to cost him. Jesus is never late. It also shows that Jesus notices the people no one else notices. And look at his words, don't be afraid, just believe. He says that to anyone facing the hardest news. Notice too that he took her by the hand and told them to give her something to eat. He is both powerful and tender.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus has authority over sickness and death, and that he treats the high and the low with the same love."""
